WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.440
In the last video, where I discussed how the&nbsp;
Delian league was formed, I mentioned that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.680
Ionians were impressed by Aristedes quick and just&nbsp;
actions against the conspiracy within the Hellenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:18.320
army before the battle of Plataea in 479 BCE.&nbsp;
This event confirmed to the Ionians that Aristides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.480
reputation of being the quote unquote “just&nbsp;
Aristides” was accurate, and so, a year later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.080
when it came to deciding how much will each city&nbsp;
pay to the treasury of the newly formed Delian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.160
League, Aristedes was chosen by the allies, who&nbsp;
where mostly Ionians, to conduct ‘the assessments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.400
according to each member's worth and ability to&nbsp;
pay.’ According to a Roman era historian Diodorus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:50.000
Aristides ‘distributed the sum so accurately and&nbsp;
justly that all the cities consented to it.’ This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.920
overly positive view of Aristides assessment could&nbsp;
be Athenian propaganda at play, however, from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.520
sources it does seem like a lot of the city states&nbsp;
did initially go into the Delian League willingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.680
which they probably wouldn’t have if the initially&nbsp;
determined payments were exorbitantly high.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.800
The total initial sum of the payments to the&nbsp;
treasury, at least according to Thucydedis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.520
turned out to be 460 talents a year. To put that&nbsp;
into perspective, there are 6,000 drachmi in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.600
talent and it is said that a citizen could easily&nbsp;
sustain himself with half a drachma a day and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.437 --> 00:01:40.797
just an ideal number the Athenians came up&nbsp;
with, when in reality the money that got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.797 --> 00:01:45.917
eventually collected by the Hellenotamiai,&nbsp;
the Athenian treasures, was smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:51.837
as the ideal Athenian number didn’t account&nbsp;
for cities defaulting on the payments or, being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:58.557
unable to pay the full amount or, even refusing&nbsp;
to pay the full amount, or paying late, etc.

00:01:58.557 --> 00:02:04.797
Going back to Thucydides, he also mentioned&nbsp;
that not all states paid into the treasury. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:08.637
most states, in lieu of not&nbsp;
sending their own citizens to war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.637 --> 00:02:13.117
paid into the treasury, however, some&nbsp;
city states, like Samos for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:17.597
contributed ships and soldiers to the League&nbsp;
rather than money. The city states that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.597 --> 00:02:23.517
contributed ships and soldiers rather than money&nbsp;
saw this as a point of pride, they didn’t have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:28.477
pay into the treasury for they were at the front&nbsp;
lines fighting the Persians with the Athenians.

00:02:29.117 --> 00:02:33.677
Now that the payments and military obligations&nbsp;
of the newly formed league were sorted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.677 --> 00:02:38.077
Athens’ first order of business was to&nbsp;
decide who should be in charge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:43.197
league’s military expeditions. Since Athens&nbsp;
was designated as the Delian League’s hegemon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.197 --> 00:02:47.997
it was the responsibility of the Athenians&nbsp;
to appoint a leader for the leagues military.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.997 --> 00:02:52.877
As you may recall, the last person in charge&nbsp;
of the combined Hellenic League’s military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.877 --> 00:02:57.517
which preceded the Delian League, was&nbsp;
the hated Spartan general Pausanias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.517 --> 00:03:01.677
who eventually got booted out by the&nbsp;
Greek city states for his behaviour.

00:03:01.677 --> 00:03:06.957
A quick side note here about Pausanias. He&nbsp;
wasn’t very liked in Sparta at this point because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.957 --> 00:03:12.797
not only did he lose them the leadership of the&nbsp;
Hellenic League but there were also many rumours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.797 --> 00:03:18.317
about him trying to incite a slave rebellion in&nbsp;
Sparta all while being allied with the Persian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:24.797
king. With that said, Pausanias was safe for now&nbsp;
as these were just rumours with no real evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.797 --> 00:03:30.477
but then, one day, one of these rumours got&nbsp;
substantiated by a messenger who claimed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.477 --> 00:03:35.997
be highered by Pausanias to deliver a message&nbsp;
to the Persian King. This, combined with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.997 --> 00:03:41.357
already circulating rumours, didn’t look good&nbsp;
for Pausanias and so when he found out that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.357 --> 00:03:46.717
messenger conspired against him he knew that he&nbsp;
was in trouble and, therefore, quickly, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.717 --> 00:03:52.477
he could be arrested, ran into a nearby Temple&nbsp;
of Athena in Sparta. Now the Greeks couldn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.477 --> 00:03:59.117
well more accurately shouldn’t, arrest someone&nbsp;
in a temple who asked for sanctuary, as the said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.117 --> 00:04:04.477
person was deemed to be under the protection of&nbsp;
the god the temple was enshrined to. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:09.917
the Spartan officials just stood outside of the&nbsp;
temple hoping to wait out Pausanias. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.917 --> 00:04:14.557
to Diodorus, Pausanias’ mother showed&nbsp;
up at the scene, with a brick in hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.557 --> 00:04:20.077
and placed it in front of the entrance while&nbsp;
stating ‘You are unworthy to be a Spartan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.077 --> 00:04:25.597
you are not my son’. The Spartan officials&nbsp;
then followed her lead and placed more bricks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.597 --> 00:04:31.437
in front of the entrance until Pausanias was&nbsp;
completely walled off inside the Temple. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:37.197
a while Pausanias starved to death. This&nbsp;
act was un-welcomed by the goddess Athena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.197 --> 00:04:42.317
as the Spartans, even though indirectly,&nbsp;
just killed someone who was supposed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.317 --> 00:04:48.237
under Athenas protection. Athena, through&nbsp;
the oracle at Delphi, voiced her anger at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.237 --> 00:04:54.477
Spartans. To apiece Athena, the Spartans buried&nbsp;
Pausanias in the consecrated grounds of the temple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.477 --> 00:05:00.237
where he died and erected two bronze statues in&nbsp;
the temple “and dedicated them as a substitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.237 --> 00:05:06.077
for Pausanias’ [death].” Angering the patron god&nbsp;
of your biggest rival perhaps wasn’t the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.077 --> 00:05:10.717
tactic for the Spartans as the Athenians&nbsp;
continued to grow in power during this time.

00:05:11.997 --> 00:05:15.197
Back in Athens.
Themistocles was trying to control most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.197 --> 00:05:20.717
of Athenian politics, which also likely included&nbsp;
the military leadership of the Delian League.&nbsp;

00:05:20.717 --> 00:05:26.797
However, Aristides, ever the rival to&nbsp;
Themistocles, championed his ally named Cimon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.797 --> 00:05:32.157
as the new head of the league's military. Cimon&nbsp;
was well known in Athens, he was the son of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.157 --> 00:05:38.237
famous Miltiades, and very successfully partook&nbsp;
in the various battles of the Greco Persian Wars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:43.437
Plus, the fact that Aristedes was so well liked&nbsp;
by the other members of the Delian League meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.437 --> 00:05:49.037
that many Athenians saw him as the reason why&nbsp;
Athens ended up in such a politically lucrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.037 --> 00:05:55.197
position as the leaders of the Delian League and,&nbsp;
therefore, Aristides’ championing of Cimon swayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.197 --> 00:06:00.877
a lot of votes towards Cimon’s way. In the end,&nbsp;
unsurprisingly, Cimon became the new leader of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.877 --> 00:06:05.837
the League’s military over Themistocles and&nbsp;
the Greek attacks upon Persia could resume.

00:06:05.837 --> 00:06:11.837
The first expedition of the league was in c. 476&nbsp;
against the city of Eion which was one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:16.877
important Persian strongholds in Thrace. Cimon&nbsp;
defeated the local Persian leader in a battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.877 --> 00:06:22.317
and then besieged the city. After a while the&nbsp;
local Persian leader saw that there was no hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.317 --> 00:06:28.637
of victory for him and, in an act of desperation,&nbsp;
layed fire to the city and then committed suicide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.637 --> 00:06:34.077
Cimon tried to salvage as much of the burning city&nbsp;
as possible and enslaved most of its population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.077 --> 00:06:39.037
It is important to understand here, and for the&nbsp;
rest of the video series, that people were often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.037 --> 00:06:44.877
seen as part of the booty that could be gotten&nbsp;
in a war. A slave was a commodity that could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.877 --> 00:06:51.270
used or sold just like metals or grains, and&nbsp;
if the captured slave happened to be a high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:56.877
ranking person, you could even sell them back&nbsp;
to their family for a high ransom. So capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.877 --> 00:07:02.877
slaves in a war was very valuable. In fact&nbsp;
there’s an anecdote about Cimon and slaves.

00:07:02.877 --> 00:07:08.157
According to Plutarch. After one particular&nbsp;
battle where the Athenians and the allies managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.157 --> 00:07:13.677
to capture a lot of Persians, Cimon stripped&nbsp;
the Persians of all their valuables and placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.677 --> 00:07:19.837
the valuables on a pile next to the Persians.&nbsp;
Then he turned to the allies and asked them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.837 --> 00:07:25.677
pick which they wanted. All the scraggly&nbsp;
looking Persians or their “gold anklets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.677 --> 00:07:32.317
armlets and collars and jackets and purple robes&nbsp;
for display”. The allies without hesitation chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.317 --> 00:07:37.677
the valuables of the Persians while the Athenian&nbsp;
got the Persians themselves. The Athenians were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.677 --> 00:07:43.597
extremely unhappy with Cimon as they saw his&nbsp;
distribution of booty as completely unbalanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.597 --> 00:07:49.117
but then, after a couple of months. Families&nbsp;
of the enslaved Persians came to Athens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.117 --> 00:07:55.037
asking to buyout their family members, each and&nbsp;
every one of them. After the deals were made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.037 --> 00:08:01.037
it turned out that the amount of money the&nbsp;
Athenians made was far greater than the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.037 --> 00:08:06.717
gold and silver the allies got, and so Cimon&nbsp;
was praised in Athens as a cunning genius.

00:08:06.717 --> 00:08:11.997
Anyways, back to Eion. After this fairly&nbsp;
violent capture of Eion, where much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.997 --> 00:08:17.037
population was enslaved, other Threcian cities&nbsp;
surrendered to the league fairly quickly.

00:08:17.037 --> 00:08:21.277
The loot acquired from this expedition&nbsp;
was divided among the allies, and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.277 --> 00:08:26.237
aparche was given to the gods as per the&nbsp;
foundation agreement of the league, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.237 --> 00:08:32.397
the city of Eion itself, and the surrounding&nbsp;
area, was retained under direct Athenian control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.397 --> 00:08:37.197
This probably didn’t sit well with many of&nbsp;
the league members as the whole foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.197 --> 00:08:42.317
point of the league was to protect existing&nbsp;
Greek cities from Persian attacks and free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.317 --> 00:08:48.157
other Greek cities from Persian control. But here,&nbsp;
on one of the first expeditions of the league,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.157 --> 00:08:54.717
Athens was already taking control of a Greek city&nbsp;
which it quote unquote “freed” from the Persians.

00:08:54.717 --> 00:08:59.277
Likely the most angered city state about&nbsp;
this was Thasos which, before the Persian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.277 --> 00:09:03.997
conquest of Thrace, controlled Eion and&nbsp;
the surrounding area as it’s peraia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.997 --> 00:09:08.477
basically a colony, but now that the&nbsp;
area was liberated from the Persians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.477 --> 00:09:13.917
Athenians didn’t give it back to Thasos, they&nbsp;
just kept it. There were several very obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.917 --> 00:09:18.477
economic reasons for why Athenians decided&nbsp;
to keep control of this area for themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.477 --> 00:09:24.077
despite the harrumphing it caused in the league.&nbsp;
First the control of the area gave Athens access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.077 --> 00:09:29.437
to the Thracian markets through the Strymon&nbsp;
river. Second the area had ample supply of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.437 --> 00:09:35.117
Threcian timber which was absolutely necessary&nbsp;
for the large Athenian shipbuilding projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.117 --> 00:09:39.997
Attica was kind of devoid of good shipbuilding&nbsp;
lumber at this point. And third, the “access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.997 --> 00:09:45.277
precious metals from the mines around Mount&nbsp;
Pangaion” was just too valuable to give up.

00:09:45.277 --> 00:09:50.717
Right after this expedition in Thrace, Athens&nbsp;
turned its sight on the island of Scyros.

00:09:50.717 --> 00:09:53.597
The reasons for Athens&nbsp;
attacking Skyros were multiple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.157 --> 00:09:59.197
For one it layed close to a strategically&nbsp;
important trade route between Athens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.197 --> 00:10:03.757
and the Black Sea and the island also&nbsp;
harboured a lot of annoying pirates which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.757 --> 00:10:08.157
not just Athens, but also the rest of the&nbsp;
cities in the league wanted to get rid of.

00:10:08.157 --> 00:10:13.517
However, there was also a religious aspect&nbsp;
for this Athenian Expedition. The Athenians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.517 --> 00:10:19.997
at some point in the past, received an oracle from&nbsp;
Delphi bidding them to bring back “the bones of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.997 --> 00:10:26.957
Theseus to the city and honor him as befit of a&nbsp;
hero” for it is their fault that he ran away to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.957 --> 00:10:32.237
Skyros and died. If you don’t know the specific&nbsp;
myth of Theseus that is being referred to here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.237 --> 00:10:38.557
basically. Long ago, Theseus was the king of&nbsp;
Athens who, along with his friend Pirithous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.557 --> 00:10:44.077
went into the underworld to try to quote&nbsp;
unquote “rescue” Persephone. Persephone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.077 --> 00:10:48.717
was the goddess of grains and crops who&nbsp;
got abducted by Hades in order that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.717 --> 00:10:52.957
could marry her. We don’t have time to get&nbsp;
into the details of this myth but in the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.957 --> 00:10:58.397
Theseus and Pirithous were unsuccessful&nbsp;
and Theseus got trapped in the underworld&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.397 --> 00:11:05.357
where he stayed until Heracles saved him during&nbsp;
his 12th task. When Theseus returned from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.357 --> 00:11:11.517
underworld he found Athens in an active rebellion&nbsp;
against his rule and subsequently got dethroned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.517 --> 00:11:17.197
his enemies. Theseus had no choice but to flee&nbsp;
Athens for the island of Skyros which he hoped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.197 --> 00:11:24.157
to use as a base for his plan to retake Athens.&nbsp;
However, the king of Skyros was afraid of Theseus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.157 --> 00:11:29.437
and distrusted him, therefore, through deceit&nbsp;
hidden behind charming hospitality the king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.437 --> 00:11:35.357
of Skyros managed to lure Theseus into a trap&nbsp;
and kill him. Hence the bones of Theseus, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.357 --> 00:11:40.957
the Athenians have been told by the oracle to&nbsp;
retrieve, were somewhere on the island of Scyros.

00:11:40.957 --> 00:11:45.837
Now, a reasonable person might think it is a&nbsp;
bit ridiculous to attack an island because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.837 --> 00:11:51.437
allegedly has some bones of a mythical Athenian&nbsp;
figure, but you have to realize that in Ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.437 --> 00:11:56.957
Greece the Greek myths, we read today for&nbsp;
entertainment, were a religion to the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.957 --> 00:12:03.357
Greeks. People believed these things happened,&nbsp;
they followed them, literally, religiously. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.357 --> 00:12:09.997
like bones of saints in medieval Europe, bones&nbsp;
of Theseus were a big deal to ancient Athenians.

00:12:09.997 --> 00:12:15.277
With that said, as mentioned before, there were&nbsp;
also plenty of other more practical reasons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.277 --> 00:12:21.997
why Athens decided to conquer Skyros, which they&nbsp;
did under the leadership of Cimon in c. 476.

00:12:21.997 --> 00:12:27.277
Plutarch tells us that when Cimon conquered&nbsp;
Skyros he was having a very hard time finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.277 --> 00:12:33.197
the bones of Theseus. However, during one of the&nbsp;
searches for the bones, Cimon spotted an eagle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.197 --> 00:12:39.117
which landed on a suspiciously looking mound of&nbsp;
dirt. Cimon realized this must have been a sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.117 --> 00:12:44.957
from the gods, and so started digging into the&nbsp;
mound and, there, found the bones of Theseus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.957 --> 00:12:51.357
“When these relics were brought home on Theseus’&nbsp;
trireme by Cimon, the Athenians were delighted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.357 --> 00:12:57.437
and received them with splendid processions&nbsp;
and sacrifices, as though Theseus himself were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.437 --> 00:13:02.717
returning to his city.” Here, the mentioned&nbsp;
“Theseus’ trireme”, better known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.717 --> 00:13:08.077
“Ship of Theseus”, was an actual ship the&nbsp;
Athenians had in their city. It was a bronze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.077 --> 00:13:14.717
age trireme believed by the Athenians to be the&nbsp;
original ship of Theseus and, therefore, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.717 --> 00:13:20.637
maintained the ship in working sailing condition&nbsp;
for centuries. So, this bronze age trireme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.637 --> 00:13:26.477
maintained in working condition by Athenians for&nbsp;
centuries, was used by Cimon to bring the bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.477 --> 00:13:32.397
of Theseus back to Athens. This must have been&nbsp;
a very momentous occasion for the Athenians.

00:13:32.957 --> 00:13:37.837
Going back to Skyros. The inhabitants of&nbsp;
the island were either killed, enslaved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.837 --> 00:13:43.837
or driven out by the Athenians, after which the&nbsp;
Athenians set up their own colony on the island.

00:13:43.837 --> 00:13:50.717
After Skyros, in c. 472, Cimon attacked and&nbsp;
conquered Carystus on Euboea. This attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.717 --> 00:13:56.077
was a continuation of Athenian efforts dating&nbsp;
back to the 6th century to subjugate the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.077 --> 00:14:02.077
island. The island was a natural barrier to any&nbsp;
northern attack on Attica and, therefore, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.077 --> 00:14:07.837
Athenians were very keen on taking control of it.&nbsp;
They would succeed in their goals of conquering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.837 --> 00:14:12.877
the whole island at some point in the following&nbsp;
decade. It is often very hard to pinpoint exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.877 --> 00:14:18.157
dates in classical antiquity, hence I always use&nbsp;
the word circa before almost every date I say.

00:14:18.157 --> 00:14:22.717
While these military expeditions lead&nbsp;
by Cimon were occuring in the 470s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.717 --> 00:14:27.197
Themistocles and Aristides continued&nbsp;
governing Athens as the two most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.197 --> 00:14:31.597
prominent, and at this point also&nbsp;
senior, Athenian politicians.

00:14:31.597 --> 00:14:37.197
Aristides, during this time, was mainly involved&nbsp;
with the politics of the Delian League since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.197 --> 00:14:41.677
as mentioned, he was very well liked&nbsp;
by the allies but, with that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.677 --> 00:14:47.117
he seems to have not been as active in politics&nbsp;
as before because he had to deal with living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.117 --> 00:14:53.117
poverty. This was due to the fact that Aristides&nbsp;
lost a great deal of his wealth during the Persian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.117 --> 00:14:58.557
destruction of Athens in the Greco Persian&nbsp;
Wars. According to Plutarch when Aristides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.557 --> 00:15:03.437
nearing the end of his life, was asked&nbsp;
about his state of wealth, he said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.437 --> 00:15:08.557
“It became him better to be proud of his poverty&nbsp;
than Callias of his wealth; since there are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.557 --> 00:15:13.997
to be seen that make a good or bad use of&nbsp;
riches, but it is difficult, comparatively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.997 --> 00:15:19.837
to meet with one who supports poverty in a noble&nbsp;
spirit; those only should be ashamed of it who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.837 --> 00:15:24.797
incurred it against their wills.” Basically&nbsp;
saying that he lost his wealth willingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.797 --> 00:15:30.317
serving Athens, and so he is not ashamed of&nbsp;
living in poverty. Aristides then died most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.317 --> 00:15:39.037
likely in Athens in c. 468 which was, however, not&nbsp;
before the ostracism of Themistocles in c. 471.

00:15:39.037 --> 00:15:43.597
Before the ostracism, Themistocles&nbsp;
continued with his pro maritime policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.157 --> 00:15:49.117
first he pushed for tax cuts to the merchants&nbsp;
and artisans so that Athens could become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.117 --> 00:15:56.237
great mercantile city, and second he stressed that&nbsp;
Athens should build at least 20 military triremes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.237 --> 00:16:02.877
a year to keep up its maritime dominance. However,&nbsp;
he also suggested, after the Greco Persian Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.877 --> 00:16:07.997
that Athens should destroy the fleets of all the&nbsp;
allied cities while they were defenceless and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.997 --> 00:16:15.357
anchored in a port. If this happened Athens could&nbsp;
finally rule all the seas unchallenged. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.357 --> 00:16:21.517
this was refused by the Athenians and such drastic&nbsp;
suggestions didn’t make him many friends. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.517 --> 00:16:26.477
also hated by the Spartans for that whole trickery&nbsp;
over the wall building project and some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.477 --> 00:16:31.757
stuff, the Delian League members didn’t like him&nbsp;
either who preferred Aristides, and over time even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.757 --> 00:16:37.917
the Athenian populace started to find Themistocles&nbsp;
too arrogant; and what do you with arrogant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.917 --> 00:16:44.717
politicians in Athens? You ostracise them;&nbsp;
and so in c. 471 Themistocles was ostracized.

00:16:45.357 --> 00:16:51.757
At some point after Themistocles ostracism and&nbsp;
around Aristides’ death, between c. 470 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.757 --> 00:16:57.357
467 the first rebellion of the Delian&nbsp;
League occurred. Naxians living on Naxos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.357 --> 00:17:01.997
the largest island of the Cyclades, decided&nbsp;
they had enough of the Delian League and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.997 --> 00:17:07.917
left. Why did they decide to leave? We don’t&nbsp;
really know. Most likely the Naxians didn’t feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.917 --> 00:17:13.597
like it was worth paying the tribute or supplying&nbsp;
ships and soldiers to a league which increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.597 --> 00:17:19.517
seemed like it was solely runned by Athens for&nbsp;
Athenian benefit. Plus there was no precedent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.517 --> 00:17:25.677
on whether a city state could leave the league&nbsp;
or not, no one had tried it before. But fun fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.237 --> 00:17:31.517
No. The Athenians decided you can’t leave the&nbsp;
league without their consent and so Athens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.517 --> 00:17:36.477
besieged and conquered Naxos. A former&nbsp;
founding ally of the league. Afterwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.477 --> 00:17:40.637
the Athenians took down the Naxian walls,&nbsp;
forced them to pay higher tribute to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.637 --> 00:17:45.757
Delian treasury and settled some Athenians on&nbsp;
the island to discourage any further descent.

00:17:46.477 --> 00:17:50.957
And so slowly but surely, under&nbsp;
the military leadership of Cimon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.957 --> 00:17:54.877
the Delian League was starting to&nbsp;
look more like an Athenian arche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.877 --> 00:17:59.037
than a League of equal city states&nbsp;
formed to fight a bigger Persian enemy.

